Creating a detailed drawing of a unicorn in TikZ can be quite complex, but I can provide you with a basic example to get you started. This example will create a simple outline of a unicorn, including its body, head, horn, and legs. You can then expand upon this to add more details.

Here's a basic TikZ code to draw a unicorn:

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
    % Body
    \fill[gray!50] (0,0) .. controls (1,1) and (2,1) .. (3,0) .. controls (4,-1) and (5,-1) .. (6,0) -- (6,2) -- (0,2) -- cycle;

    % Head
    \fill[gray!50] (2.5,2) .. controls (2.5,3) and (3.5,3) .. (3.5,2) .. controls (3.5,1) and (2.5,1) .. (2.5,2) -- cycle;

    % Horn
    \fill[gray!50] (3,3) -- (3.5,3.5) -- (3,4) -- cycle;

    % Eye
    \fill[black] (3,2.7) circle (0.1);

    % Legs
    \fill[gray!50] (1,0) -- (1,-1) -- (2,-1) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
    \fill[gray!50] (5,0) -- (5,-1) -- (4,-1) -- (4,0) -- cycle;

    % Tail
    \fill[gray!50] (6,0) .. controls (7,1) and (8,1) .. (9,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

This code will produce a simple, basic unicorn. To make it more detailed, you can add more control points to the curves, add shading, and include more features like a mane, a more detailed horn, and a more expressive face. You can also change the colors and add more layers to create a more realistic or stylized look.

To compile this code, you need to have a LaTeX distribution installed on your computer, such as TeX Live or MiKTeX, and a PDF viewer to see the result. Save the code in a `.tex` file and compile it using a LaTeX editor or command line tools like `pdflatex`.
